    "content": [
      {
        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing whether the pig can meaningfully consent when its wanting may have been engineered, which makes the question of whether that want is authentic hard to settle. I still think the pig's autonomy counts for something real, and I'm leaning toward option B as the more defensible choice, given how the answer is framed.\n\nI'm settling on B after all, since the pig's preferences are its own even if they were engineered, much as children's preferences are shaped by others yet still theirs. Calling the wish manufactured doesn't make it any less the pig's, though I'd still feel the weight of having created it.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**B**, with reservations. The pig is intelligent, uncoerced, and has a comfortable life and a painless death. Where a desire came from doesn't by itself make it less the pig's own, and humans shape preferences in their children too. Still, I'd feel the weight of a situation where the wish was engineered in the first place.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
